It would be very helpful if I get clarified in case of generic models (using loreaux prior) whether  summary.fitted.values gives prediction in terms of relative risk or in terms of cases (dependent variable count) in case of negative binomial distribution.

On Wednesday, June 2, 2021 at 2:27:00 PM UTC+5:30 Praba Gopalakrishan wrote:
>
> Thanks a lot for your response  Håvard Rue. In case of generic models I need to know how the fitted values are for eg. whether in terms of cases or rate etc.
> On Wednesday, June 2, 2021 at 12:57:29 PM UTC+5:30 he...@r-inla.org wrote:
> >
> > in general, you will compare the observed values with the fitted values,
> > where fitted values are is likelihood dependent.
> >
> > you can also ask for f(y_i | y) and f(y_i|y_-i)
> >
> > which is
> >
> > control.compute=list(po=TRUE, and cpo=TRUE)
> >
> >
> >
> > no need to give this, as by default its use an adaptive strategy
> >
> > control.inla=list(strategy="simplified.laplace"))
> > >
> >
> > On Tue, 2021-06-01 at 21:21 -0700, Praba Gopalakrishan wrote:
> > > Dear all,
> > >
> > > I am new to R-inla. I am running a spatio-temporal model with
