>>>>>>>>>> Dear INLA group,
>>>>>>>>>> Is there a way to limit the run-time of a call to
>>>>>>>>>> inla()?
>>>>>>>>>> My student and I are running some simulation studies.
>>>>>>>>>> Occasionally we simulate wacky, unrealistic data sets
>>>>>>>>>> that
>>>>>>>>>> either create a lot of errors (we know how to flag
>>>>>>>>>> that) or
>>>>>>>>>> cause INLA to run for a really long time. Is there a
>>>>>>>>>> way to
>>>>>>>>>> force INLA to time out after some period of time?
>>>>>>>>>> This post:
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>> https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/pA1lqfMpceE/m/MnQxfo0cAgAJ
>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> discusses a time-out for building a mesh. The
>>>>>>>>>> workaround
>>>>>> there
>>>>>>>>>> is based on the evalWithTimeout, which is now
>>>>>>>>>> deprecated in
>>>>>>>>>> favour of withTimeout. The docs of withTimeout state
>>>>>>>>>> that
>>>>>> there
>>>>>>>>>> are some incompatibilities with R functions that
>>>>>>>>>> ultimately
>>>>>> call
>>>>>>>>>> out via system() or system2(), like inla(…). According
>>>>>>>>>> to
>>>>>> the
>>>>>>>>>> linked docs, there is now an option to timeout within
>>>>>>>>>> system
>>>>>> and
>>>>>>>>>> system2.
>>>>>>>>>> We tested the attached example, and withTimout works
>>>>>>>>>> on
>>>>>> Windows
>>>>>>>>>> but not on MacOS or Linux. By ‘works’ I mean that INLA
>>>>>>>>>> is
>>>>>>>>>> interrupted. The example takes ~ 13s and we ask R to
>>>>>>>>>> time it
>>>>>> out
>>>>>>>>>> at 10s. On Windows, we get the following error (which
>>>>>>>>>> is
>>>>>> what we
>>>>>>>>>> want):
>>>>>>>>>> maxld= -605.447 fn= 33 theta= 5.074 0.729 [5.4, 0.41]
>>>>>>>>>> maxld= -605.444 fn= 41 theta= 5.070 0.728 [5.4, 0.35]
>>>>>>>>>> Error in system2(inla.call, args = paste(all.args,
>>>>>>>>>> shQuote(file.ini)),  :
>>>>>>>>>>   reached elapsed time limit
>>>>>>>>>> Error in inla.inlaprogram.has.crashed() :
>>>>>>>>>>   The inla-program exited with an error. Unless you
>>>>>> interupted
>>>>>>>>>> it yourself, please rerun with verbose=TRUE and check
>>>>>>>>>> the
>>>>>> output
>>>>>>>>>> carefully.
>>>>>>>>>>   If this does not help, please contact the developers
>>>>>>>>>> at
>>>>>>>>>> <he...@r-inla.org>.
>>>>>>>>>> On a MacOS, INLA keeps running in the background, and
>>>>>>>>>> only
>>>>>> after
>>>>>>>>>> it is finished do we get a warning that INLA took
>>>>>>>>>> longer
>>>>>> than
>>>>>>>>>> the timeout we specified:
>>>>>>>>>> Approx inference:  12.572 seconds
>>>>>>>>>> [1.9|0.0|96.8|0.8|0.5]%
>>>>>>>>>> Output          :   0.508 seconds
>>>>>>>>>> ---------------------------------
>>>>>>>>>> Total           :  13.142 seconds
>>>>>>>>>> Number of fn-calls= 80 with 0.157 sec/fn-call
>>>>>>>>>> Parallel efficiency for 'Preparations' and 'Approx
>>>>>> inference':
>>>>>>>>>>     Accumulated CPU-time is equivalent to 1.78 threads
>>>>>> running
>>>>>>>>>> at 100%
>>>>>>>>>>     Efficiency using 4 threads = 44.39%
>>>>>>>>>> Error in dir(results.dir) : reached elapsed time limit
>>>>>>>>>>
>>>>>>>>>> From my read of the code, INLA uses system() on
>>>>>>>>>> Linux/MacOS
>>>>>> and
>>>>>>>>>> system2() on Windows, so I guess this could be the
>>>>>>>>>> cause of
>>>>>> the
>>>>>>>>>> difference in behaviour (sorry, I don’t know enough
>>>>>>>>>> about
>>>>>> how
>>>>>>>>>> computers work to figure that out myself!). Ultimately
>>>>>>>>>> we
>>>>>> would
>>>>>>>>>> be working in a Linux environment, so ideally, we
>>>>>>>>>> would have
>>>>>> the
>>>>>>>>>> Windows behaviour but on Linux.
>>>>>>>>>> I should say, we more or less know why we occasionally
>>>>>>>>>> get
>>>>>> wacky
>>>>>>>>>> realisations for certain settings of the simulation.
>>>>>>>>>> We plan
>>>>>> to
>>>>>>>>>> keep track of the number of times we run into this or
>>>>>>>>>> other
>>>>>>>>>> issues, but we aren’t looking for advice on how to
>>>>>>>>>> prevent
>>>>>> the
>>>>>>>>>> wacky realisations.
>>>>>>>>>> Thanks,
>>>>>>>>>> Theresa
